window.docContents[44].resolve({"/Subgraphs/#section-building-partial-colourings":{"contents":"Fundamentally we are interested in partial colourings as a tool to build total\ncolourings, so we want to be able to turn a G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ into a\nG.Coloring γ.\n\nA G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ is a G.Coloring γ./-- A G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ is a G.Coloring γ. -/\n@[simps]\ndef ColoringOn.toColoring (C : G.ColoringOn γ Set.univ) : G.Coloring γ :=\n    ⟨C, fun h ↦ C.valid (by simpa using h)⟩\n\n\nA colouring gives rise to a partial colouring on any set./-- A colouring gives rise to a partial colouring on any set. -/\n@[simps]\ndef Coloring.toColoringOn (C : G.Coloring γ) (s : Set V) : G.ColoringOn γ s :=\n    ⟨C, fun h ↦ C.valid (by simpa using h.2.2)⟩\n\n\nA graph is n-colourable if and only if G.ColorableOn n Set.univ.\n\nvariable (G)\nlemma colorableOn_univ_iff_colorable (n : ℕ) :\n    G.ColorableOn n (Set.univ : Set V) ↔ G.Colorable n := by\n  constructor <;> intro ⟨c⟩\n  · exact ⟨c.toColoring⟩\n  · exact ⟨c.toColoringOn _⟩\n\n\nThere are a few basic partial colourings that we will need to construct: the partial\ncolouring of the empty set, the partial colouring of a singleton, and the partial\ncolouring of a pair of non-adjacent vertices.\n\nThe partial colouring of the empty set by a colour c.variable {G}\n/-- The partial colouring of the empty set by a colour c. -/\n@[simps!]\ndef ColoringOn.empty (c : γ) : G.ColoringOn γ ∅ :=\n  Coloring.mk (fun _ => c) fun h => h.1.elim\n\n\nThe partial colouring of the singleton set {a} by a colour c./-- The partial colouring of the singleton set {a} by a colour c. -/\n@[simps]\ndef ColoringOn.singleton (a : V) (c : γ) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ {a} where\n  toFun := fun _ ↦ c\n  map_rel' := by simp\n\n\nThe partial colouring of a pair of non-adjacent vertices by a colour c./-- The partial colouring of a pair of non-adjacent vertices by a colour c. -/\n@[simps]\ndef ColoringOn.ofNotAdj {a b : V} (h : ¬ G.Adj a b) (c : γ) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ {a, b} where\n  toFun := fun _ ↦ c\n  map_rel' := by\n    rintro x y ⟨hx, hy, hxy⟩ -\n    grind [adj_comm]\n\n\nWe can combine two partial colourings by extending the first using the second,\nprovided they are compatible in the sense that there is no edge between a vertex\ncoloured by C₁ and a vertex coloured only by C₂ using the same colour. This gives\na partial colouring on the union of the two sets.\n\nWe can combine colourings C₁ of s and C₂ of t if they are compatible,\ni.e. ∀ v w, v ∈ s → w ∈ t \\ s → G.Adj v w → C₁ v ≠ C₂ w to get a colouring of\ns ∪ t. It agrees with C₁ on s and with C₂ on t \\ s./-- We can combine colourings C₁ of s and C₂ of t if they are compatible,\ni.e. ∀ v w, v ∈ s → w ∈ t \\ s → G.Adj v w → C₁ v ≠ C₂ w to get a colouring of\ns ∪ t. It agrees with C₁ on s and with C₂ on t \\ s. -/\n@[simps]\ndef ColoringOn.union [DecidablePred (· ∈ s)] (C₁ : G.ColoringOn γ s)\n    (C₂ : G.ColoringOn γ t)\n    (h : ∀ ⦃v w⦄, v ∈ s → w ∈ t \\ s → G.Adj v w → C₁ v ≠ C₂ w) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ (s ∪ t) where\n  toFun := fun v ↦ ite (v ∈ s) (C₁ v) (C₂ v)\n  map_rel' := by\n    rintro v w ⟨hv', hw', hadj⟩ hf\n    split_ifs at hf with hv hw hw\n    · exact C₁.valid ⟨hv, hw, hadj⟩ hf\n    · exact h hv ⟨hw'.resolve_left hw, hw⟩ hadj hf\n    · exact h hw ⟨hv'.resolve_left hv, hv⟩ hadj.symm hf.symm\n    · exact C₂.valid ⟨hv'.resolve_left hv, hw'.resolve_left hw, hadj⟩ hf\n\n\n#check ColoringOn.union_apply -- (C₁.union C₂ h) v = if v ∈ s then C₁ v else C₂ v\n\n\nNext we extend a colouring C of s to insert x s using a colour c that is not\nused by C to colour a neighbour of x in s. We could build this as\n(ColoringOn.singleton x c).union C _, followed by a copy from {x} ∪ s to\ninsert x s, but then @[simps!] would only unfold it as far as that union.\nInstead we define it directly, with the same four cases in the validity proof as for\nunion, and @[simps] then generates exactly the lemma we want.\n\nExtend C : G.ColoringOn γ s to insert x s by colouring x with a colour c\nthat C does not use on any neighbour of x in s./-- Extend C : G.ColoringOn γ s to insert x s by colouring x with a colour c\nthat C does not use on any neighbour of x in s. -/\n@[simps]\nprotected def ColoringOn.insert [DecidableEq V] (x : V) {c : γ}\n    (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) (h : ∀ ⦃v⦄, v ∈ s → G.Adj x v → C v ≠ c) :\n    G.ColoringOn γ (insert x s) where\n  toFun v := if v = x then c else C v\n  map_rel' := by\n    rintro v w ⟨hv, hw, hadj⟩ hf\n    split_ifs at hf with hvx hwx hwx\n    · exact hadj.ne (hvx.trans hwx.symm)\n    · exact h (hw.resolve_left hwx) (hvx ▸ hadj) hf.symm\n    · exact h (hv.resolve_left hvx) (hwx ▸ hadj.symm) hf\n    · exact C.valid ⟨hv.resolve_left hvx, hw.resolve_left hwx, hadj⟩ hf\n\n\nSo C.insert x h colours x with c and agrees with C elsewhere.\n\n#check ColoringOn.insert_apply -- (C.insert x h) v = if v = x then c else C v\n\n\nFinally we can relabel the colours of a partial colouring along any injective map (in\nparticular along any permutation of the colours): validity is preserved, since an\ninjection cannot merge two distinct colours.\n\nRelabel the colours of a partial colouring along an embedding./-- Relabel the colours of a partial colouring along an embedding. -/\n@[simps!]\ndef ColoringOn.map {β : Type*} (f : γ ↪ β) (C : G.ColoringOn γ s) :\n    G.ColoringOn β s :=\n  (Embedding.completeGraph f).toHom.comp C\n\n\n#check ColoringOn.map_apply --  (ColoringOn.map f C) x = f (C x)\n\nend partcol\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\nsection\nnamespace SimpleGraph\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Partial Colourings*","header":"9.6.2. Building Partial Colourings","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-building-partial-colourings"},"/Theorems/#section-mantel-s-theorem":{"contents":"The case r = 2 of Turán's theorem is Mantel's theorem W. Mantel, Vraagstuk XXVIII [Problem 28], Wiskundige Opgaven 10 (1907), 60–61. Solution by H. Gouwentak, W. Mantel, J. Teixeira de Mattes, F. Schuh and W. A. Wythoff.: a\ntriangle-free graph on n vertices has at most ⌊n² / 4⌋ edges. Equality holds for\nthe balanced complete bipartite graph T[n, 2].\n\n#check turanNumber_two -- turanNumber n 2 = n ^ 2 / 4\n\n\nBy CliqueFree.mycielskian, the Mycielskian of a triangle-free graph on n vertices\nis a triangle-free graph on 2 * n + 1 vertices, so Mantel's theorem bounds its\nnumber of edges by (2 * n + 1) ^ 2 / 4.\n\nexample [Fintype V] [DecidableRel G.Adj] (h3 : G.CliqueFree 3) :\n    #mμ(G).edgeFinset ≤ (2 * Fintype.card V + 1) ^ 2 /4 := by\n  convert CliqueFree.card_edgeFinset_le h3.mycielskian\n  simp [two_mul, turanNumber_two]\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Theorems\u0009Turán's Theorem, Stability and Asymptotics\u0009Turán's Theorem","header":"13.2.2.1. Mantel's Theorem","id":"/Theorems/#section-mantel-s-theorem"},"/Walks/#section-the-bondy-chv-tal-theorem":{"contents":"The key observation of Bondy and Chvátal is that the degree condition above is only\never needed one pair at a time: if u and v are distinct non-adjacent vertices\nwith deg u + deg v ≥ |V|, then G is Hamiltonian iff G ⊔ edge u v is\nHamiltonian. Repeatedly adding such edges yields the closure of G.\n\nThe first step towards the Bondy–Chvátal theorem is a piece of cycle surgery. A\nHamiltonian cycle of G ⊔ edge u v may use the new edge u — v, which is not\navailable in G. Deleting u — v from such a cycle c should leave a\nHamiltonian path from u to v that does lie in G, and the previous section\nthen closes this path up into a Hamiltonian cycle of G. Hamiltonicity plays no\npart in the surgery itself, so we cut open an arbitrary cycle and record what the\nresulting path inherits from it.\n\nWe first need a simple fact about darts of a cycle c : G.Walk u u: if the first\nvertex of a dart d of c is u, then the second vertex of d is the second\nvertex of the cycle. Mathlib proves this for any walk whose Walk.dropLast is a\npath, and dropping the last dart of a cycle leaves a path.\n\n#check Walk.IsPath.snd_eq_of_mem_darts\n#check Walk.IsCycle.isPath_dropLast\n\n\nDeleting an edge u — v from a cycle c leaves a path from u to v that is one\nedge shorter than c, visits exactly the vertices of c, and avoids u — v.\n\nThe edge u — v of the cycle is the image of a dart, which either points from u\nto v or from v to u (dart_edge_eq_mk'_iff'). Rotating the cycle to be based\nat the dart's first vertex makes the dart the first dart of the cycle, and the\nreversed tail of the rotated cycle then runs from the dart's first vertex to its\nsecond. It is a path, since the tail of a cycle is a path; it visits every vertex of\nthe cycle, since the base vertex reappears at the end of the tail; and it avoids the\ndeleted first edge, since a cycle is a trail, so no edge is repeated. This is the\nrequired path in the first case, and its reverse is the required path in the second.\n\nlemma Walk.IsCycle.exists_isPath_of_mem_edges {x u v : V} {c : G.Walk x x}\n    (hc : c.IsCycle) (he : s(u, v) ∈ c.edges) :\n    ∃ p : G.Walk u v, p.IsPath ∧ p.length + 1 = c.length ∧\n      (∀ w, w ∈ p.support ↔ w ∈ c.support) ∧ s(u, v) ∉ p.edges := by\n  classical\n  -- cutting c open at a dart from a to b gives an a-b path\n  have key : ∀ {a b : V}, ∀ d ∈ c.darts, d.fst = a → d.snd = b →\n      ∃ p : G.Walk a b, p.IsPath ∧ p.length + 1 = c.length ∧\n        (∀ w, w ∈ p.support ↔ w ∈ c.support) ∧ s(a, b) ∉ p.edges := by\n    rintro a b d hd rfl rfl\n    -- rotate c to be based at d.fst, so that d becomes its first dart\n    have ha := c.dart_fst_mem_support_of_mem_darts hd\n    have hr := hc.rotate ha\n    rw [← hr.isPath_dropLast.snd_eq_of_mem_darts\n      ((c.rotate_darts _ ha).perm.mem_iff.2 hd) rfl]\n    -- the reversed tail of the rotated cycle is the required path\n    refine ⟨_, hr.isPath_tail.reverse, ?_, fun w => ?_, ?_⟩\n    · simpa using length_tail_add_one hr.not_nil\n    · -- d.fst is dropped from the start of the support but is still the end\n      have := cons_support_tail hr.not_nil\n      grind [support_reverse, end_mem_support, mem_support_rotate_iff]\n    · -- no edge is repeated, so the first edge does not occur in the tail\n      have hnd := hr.edges_nodup\n      rw [← (c.rotate _ ha).cons_tail_eq hr.not_nil, edges_cons] at hnd\n      simpa using (List.nodup_cons.1 hnd).1\n  -- the edge u — v is the image of a dart of c\n  obtain ⟨d, hd, hde⟩ := List.mem_map.1 he\n  rcases dart_edge_eq_mk'_iff'.1 hde with ⟨hfst, hsnd⟩ | ⟨hfst, hsnd⟩\n  · -- the dart points from u to v: cut there\n    exact key d hd hfst hsnd\n  · -- the dart points from v to u: cut there and reverse the resulting path\n    obtain ⟨p, h1, h2, h3, h4⟩ := key d hd hfst hsnd\n    exact ⟨p.reverse, h1.reverse, by simpa using h2, by simpa using h3,\n      by simpa [Sym2.eq_swap] using h4⟩\n\n\nIf the cycle is Hamiltonian then the path visits every vertex, so it is a\nHamiltonian path (IsPath.isHamiltonian_iff).\n\nlemma Walk.IsHamiltonianCycle.exists_isHamiltonian_walk_of_mem_edges [DecidableEq V]\n    {x u v : V} {c : G.Walk x x} (hc : c.IsHamiltonianCycle)\n    (he : s(u, v) ∈ c.edges) :\n    ∃ p : G.Walk u v, p.IsHamiltonian ∧ s(u, v) ∉ p.edges := by\n  obtain ⟨p, hp, -, hsupp, hpe⟩ := hc.isCycle.exists_isPath_of_mem_edges he\n  exact ⟨p, hp.isHamiltonian_iff.2 fun w => (hsupp w).2 (hc.mem_support w), hpe⟩\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Hamiltonian Graphs: Ore, Dirac and Bondy–Chvátal*","header":"8.8.4. The Bondy–Chvátal Theorem","id":"/Walks/#section-the-bondy-chv-tal-theorem"}});